  "description": "Chip Authentication after BAC, id-CA-DH-AES-CBC-CMAC-128 over the RFC 5114 2048-bit/256-bit group: the command-chaining and left-aligned-keySeed shape. DG14 carries keyId 1 on both entries, so MSE:Set AT emits DO84 (84 01 01); the X9.42 dhpublicnumber SPKI carries p, g, q. DO80(y) is 260 bytes, so the 224-byte COMMAND_CHAINING_CHUNK_SIZE splits it into two GENERAL AUTHENTICATE commands. NOTE the two upstream defects this pins: SecureMessaging.maskClassAndPad masks the CLA to the literal 0C, so the 0x10 chaining bit never reaches the chip, and sendGeneralAuthenticate unwraps a DO7C from EVERY response; the simulated chip therefore reassembles by watching for a complete DO80 and answers 7C 00 to the intermediate segment, which is the idealised chip upstream's code assumes. The DH keySeed is the DH_size(p)-byte left-aligned buffer upstream composes. runners omitted = both.",
